  {
   "title": "Folds",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "folds",
     "data": {
      "items": [
       {
        "title": "Why \"ripple\"",
        "summary": "The carry moves one stage at a time",
        "body": [
         {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Each stage must wait for the carry from the stage to its right. A wave of carries moves from bit 0 to bit 3."
         },
         {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
           "4 bits: 8 gate delays in the worst case",
           "32 bits: 64 gate delays in the worst case"
          ]
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "title": "Overflow",
        "summary": "Cout = 1 means that the result has 5 bits",
        "body": [
         {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "",
          "code": "  1111   (15)\n+ 0001   ( 1)\n------\n1 0000   (16): S = 0000, Cout = 1"
         },
         {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "For unsigned numbers, Cout is the overflow flag."
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "title": "Subtraction",
        "summary": "Invert B and set Cin to 1",
        "body": [
         {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "",
          "code": "// A - B = A + (NOT B) + 1, the two's complement of B\nadder4 sub(.a(a), .b(~b), .cin(1'b1), .s(d), .cout(no_borrow));"
         },
         {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The same circuit adds and subtracts. One XOR on each B input selects the operation."
         }
        ]
       }
      ]
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Composed folds",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "folds",
     "data": {
      "items": [
       {
        "title": "Ripple-carry",
        "summary": "The simple design",
        "body": [],
        "children": [
         {
          "title": "How it works",
          "summary": "Each stage waits for the carry before it",
          "body": [
           {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "The carry goes from FA0 to FA3, one stage at a time."
           },
           {
            "type": "code",
            "lang": "",
            "code": "module adder4(input [3:0] a, b, input cin, output [3:0] s, output cout);\n  wire [4:0] c;\n  assign c[0] = cin;\n  genvar i;\n  generate for (i = 0; i < 4; i = i + 1) begin : stage\n    full_adder fa(a[i], b[i], c[i], s[i], c[i+1]);\n  end endgenerate\n  assign cout = c[4];\nendmodule"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "title": "Cost",
          "summary": "20 gates, 8 gate delays",
          "body": [
           {
            "type": "list",
            "ordered": false,
            "items": [
             "**Gates:** 5 for each bit, so 20",
             "**Delay:** 2 for each bit, so 8"
            ]
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "title": "Trade-offs",
          "summary": "Small, but slow for wide numbers",
          "body": [
           {
            "type": "list",
            "ordered": false,
            "items": [
             "**Win:** Few gates and a regular layout",
             "**Lose:** The delay grows with each bit"
            ]
           }
          ]
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "title": "Carry-lookahead",
        "summary": "The fast design",
        "body": [],
        "children": [
         {
          "title": "How it works",
          "summary": "All carries come from g and p at the same time",
          "body": [
           {
            "type": "p",
            "text": "Each bit has `g = a AND b` (it makes a carry) and `p = a XOR b` (it passes a carry)."
           },
           {
            "type": "code",
            "lang": "",
            "code": "c1 = g0 + p0·c0\nc2 = g1 + p1·g0 + p1·p0·c0\nc3 = g2 + p2·g1 + p2·p1·g0 + p2·p1·p0·c0\nc4 = g3 + p3·g2 + p3·p2·g1 + p3·p2·p1·g0 + p3·p2·p1·p0·c0"
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "title": "Cost",
          "summary": "More gates, 4 gate delays",
          "body": [
           {
            "type": "list",
            "ordered": false,
            "items": [
             "**Gates:** about 30 for 4 bits",
             "**Delay:** 4, for each width up to 4 bits"
            ]
           }
          ]
         },
         {
          "title": "Trade-offs",
          "summary": "Fast, but the gates grow quickly",
          "body": [
           {
            "type": "list",
            "ordered": false,
            "items": [
             "**Win:** The delay does not grow with each bit",
             "**Lose:** Wide AND gates. Real designs use groups of 4 bits"
            ]
           }
          ]
         }
        ]
       }
      ]
     }
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "summary": "A 4-bit ripple-carry adder adds two 4-bit numbers. It is a chain of four full adders. The carry out of each stage goes into the next stage."
}